  "articleBody": "It's incredibly frustrating when you think your roof is solid, only for a storm in Allen to reveal a leak. Texas weather can be brutal, with high winds, heavy rain, and hail, and even a small storm can exploit existing vulnerabilities or create new damage you didn't know was there. Understanding why your roof might be leaking is the first step to getting it fixed and protecting your home.\n\nOften, leaks after a storm stem from damage that isn't immediately obvious from the ground. High winds can lift or peel back shingles, even if they don't completely detach, allowing water to seep underneath. Hail can create small, seemingly insignificant dents or 'bruises' on shingles that compromise their protective granule layer over time, leading to accelerated wear and eventual leaks. Damaged or clogged gutters can also cause water to back up and overflow, getting behind fascia boards or under shingles. Flashing around chimneys, skylights, and vents is another common culprit; if it's bent, cracked, or improperly installed, it becomes a direct entry point for water. Even a small impact from a falling branch can create enough of a crack to let water in. These issues might not be visible to the untrained eye, making a professional inspection crucial to pinpoint the exact source of the leak and prevent further damage.\n\n**When Professional Guidance Helps**\nWhen you've got a leak after a storm, professional guidance is absolutely essential. Climbing onto a damaged roof yourself can be incredibly dangerous, especially if it's wet or compromised. Experts can safely identify the source of the leak, assess the full extent of storm damage – both visible and hidden – and recommend the most effective repairs. They also have the tools and experience to properly document the damage with photos and explanations, which is invaluable if you need to file an insurance claim. This kind of professional assessment helps ensure that all issues are addressed properly, giving you lasting peace of mind and preventing small leaks from turning into major structural problems.\n\nIf your Allen home is experiencing leaks after a storm, don't wait. Contact a professional for a thorough post-storm roof inspection.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}